  <revision rev="15" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>40528c3817c46c56301831d9f77d4686</srcmd5>
    <version>0.26.0</version>
    <time>1794318241</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>- Update to 0.26.0:
  Added:
  * Qweights=flat (openhtj2k_encoder::set_qfactor_weights(1)) turns off the
    visual weights of the Qfactor path, for scientific or other non-visual
    data. Every subband weight is 1 at every Qfactor, so the steps are
    MSE-optimal, as the guideline's are from Qfactor=97 up; without the MCT
    every component is independent data with unit colour gain. The Qfactor
    still sets the reference step, so a Qfactor gives larger files and smaller
    errors: on an 8-bit RGB image, 28-54% more bits and 2.6-3.8 dB more PSNR at
    Qfactor=30 to 90. The default, Qweights=visual, is unchanged.
    estimate_qfactor --weights flat recovers the Qfactor of such files.
  Changed:
  * Qfactor on sub-sampled (4:2:0 or 4:2:2) colour input follows version 2.0 of
    the JPEG Qfactor guideline, &quot;Controlling JPEG 2000 image quality using a
    single parameter (Qfactor)&quot; (ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 29/WG 1 N101553). Its chroma
    weights include the gain of upsampling the component for display, 2 for
    4:2:0 and 1.4142 for 4:2:2, on every subband including LL; the encoder used
    the version 1.0 weights, which leave it out. Below Qfactor=97 the chroma
    steps of such files are finer by up to 2 (4:2:0) or 1.4142 (4:2:2) times,
    so the files are larger: a 1024x1024 4:2:0 test image grows by 41% at
    Qfactor=30, 23% at 75 and 9% at 90. Output for gray, gray + alpha and 4:4:4
    colour input, and all output without Qfactor, is byte-identical. The
    experimental analytic weighting (Qcsf=mannos|daly) applies the same gain to
    sub-sampled components. estimate_qfactor follows the new weights, so on a
    sub-sampled file written by 0.25.0 or earlier it finds the Qfactor from the
    luminance component only and reports a mismatch on the chroma components.
  Fixed:
  * On the single-tile reuse path (invoke_line_based_stream_reuse() and
    invoke_line_based_direct() after enable_single_tile_reuse(true)), a frame
    whose headers differed from the previous frame's could decode with the</comment>
    <requestid>1396475</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="16" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>119557a579e1dacba6fa0b89d0ea8e19</srcmd5>
    <version>0.27.0</version>
    <time>1794410955</time>
    <user>dimstar_suse</user>
    <comment>- Update to 0.27.0:
  Added:
  * openhtj2k_decoder::is_truncated(): after a decode, whether the codestream
    lacked data of the image, which then holds only the data there was. It is
    true when the codestream ends before its EOC marker (a truncated file or
    download), when a tile has no data, or when a packet or a code-block's data
    runs past the bytes there are. Image libraries can use it to tell a partial
    image from a whole one.
  Fixed:
  * A codestream cut short now decodes from the data it holds, wherever the cut
    falls after the marker code of its first SOT marker segment, and one
    warning reports it (&quot;codestream ends before its EOC marker&quot;).
  * A code-block length that runs over a tile-part boundary (a malformed
    codestream) drops that code-block's data in the packet and decodes the
    rest, with the &quot;malformed input&quot; warning as before; its bytes used to be
    decoded in part, which failed the decode.</comment>
    <requestid>1396742</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="17" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>ae558864e33b9cf50aa112dfcc6ba150</srcmd5>
    <version>0.28.1</version>
    <time>1795023123</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>- Update to 0.28.1:
  * Fixes the multi-threaded WebAssembly builds, which since 0.25.0 decoded
    every frame after the first on one thread: the RTP demo, the WebTransport
    viewer and the JPIP demos ran at about half speed. The README and the
    documentation are reorganised by audience.

- Update to 0.28.0:
  * Adds the precision-scaled Qfactor for samples of more than 8 bits
    (Qscale=bits, openhtj2k_encoder::set_qfactor_scale(1)): a Qfactor removes
    the same fraction of the precision at every bit depth, so 10- to 16-bit
    data can be coded at the fidelity that the JPEG Qfactor guideline's scale
    leaves between Qfactor=99 and 100. It is opt-in.</comment>
    <requestid>1398381</requestid>
  </revision>
